WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.471
Hello!

2
00:00:00.471 --> 00:00:05.186
Today we go one step beyond ordinary differential equations.

3
00:00:05.186 --> 00:00:12.729
The unknown function now depends on two variables instead of one, for example position and time.

4
00:00:12.729 --> 00:00:24.830
After this lesson you will find the order and linearity of a partial differential equation and recognise the three main equations of mathematical physics.

5
00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.915
Take a thin fin of a CPU heat sink.

6
00:00:27.915 --> 00:00:32.450
Its temperature u depends on the point x and the time t.

7
00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:41.683
The simplest model says the derivative of u with respect to t equals the second derivative of u with respect to x.

8
00:00:41.683 --> 00:00:47.514
An equation with partial derivatives is a partial differential equation,

9
00:00:47.514 --> 00:00:51.806
and the order of its highest derivative is its order.

10
00:00:52.056 --> 00:01:01.013
An equation is linear when u and its derivatives appear to the first power, are not multiplied together,

11
00:01:01.013 --> 00:01:04.371
and have coefficients independent of u.

12
00:01:04.371 --> 00:01:12.639
Those coefficients may depend only on the independent variables, for example x and t or x and y.

13
00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:19.615
If the right side is zero, the equation is homogeneous and superposition applies.

14
00:01:19.615 --> 00:01:24.782
A term such as u times u sub x makes the equation nonlinear.

15
00:01:25.032 --> 00:01:28.867
Example: solve u sub x y equals zero.

16
00:01:28.867 --> 00:01:34.672
It says that the x derivative of u does not depend on y.

17
00:01:34.672 --> 00:01:38.715
So u sub x equals some function h of x.

18
00:01:38.715 --> 00:01:45.038
Careful: this is not a constant, it is an arbitrary function!

19
00:01:45.288 --> 00:01:47.253
Now integrate in x.

20
00:01:47.253 --> 00:01:53.976
The “constant” of integration becomes an arbitrary function of y.

21
00:01:53.976 --> 00:01:57.699
Answer: u equals f of x plus g of y.

22
00:01:57.699 --> 00:02:04.939
Check: the x derivative is f prime of x, and its y derivative is zero.

23
00:02:04.939 --> 00:02:11.558
So the general solution of a PDE depends on arbitrary functions.

24
00:02:11.808 --> 00:02:13.644
Three main equations.

25
00:02:13.644 --> 00:02:22.476
The vibrating string: the second time derivative of u equals a squared times the second x derivative,

26
00:02:22.476 --> 00:02:25.974
where a squared is tension over density.

27
00:02:25.974 --> 00:02:29.647
Heat in a rod has a first time derivative.

28
00:02:29.647 --> 00:02:41.102
In a two-dimensional steady source-free setting we obtain Laplace’s equation: the sum of the second derivatives in x and y is zero.

29
00:02:41.352 --> 00:02:50.947
The graph shows the solution e to the minus t times sine x at three times: zero, one half and one.

30
00:02:50.947 --> 00:02:58.682
This single eigenmode keeps its shape while its amplitude decays exponentially.

31
00:02:58.682 --> 00:03:07.396
A general initial profile is a sum of many modes; high-frequency variations decay faster,

32
00:03:07.396 --> 00:03:10.040
so the profile smooths out.

33
00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:16.502
For an ideal lossless string the modes do not decay but oscillate.

34
00:03:16.752 --> 00:03:18.504
Now check yourself.

35
00:03:18.504 --> 00:03:23.668
Answer the question on screen: which equation is linear?

36
00:03:23.668 --> 00:03:31.046
Remember, a function of x and y on the right-hand side does not spoil linearity.
